1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
Fuel a $100 billion a year business.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:08,000
We may rearrange his DNA in the name of culinary progress,

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
but this intelligent creature remains a trusted ally.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:14,000
He might even save your life.

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:18,000
Now, The Pig on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,000
The aroma of deep-fried foods and the shrieks from thrill

7
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:36,240
riders say it loud and clear.

8
00:00:36,340 --> 00:00:38,340
It's time for the fair.

9
00:00:38,440 --> 00:00:40,740
Surprisingly, Los Angeles, California,

10
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:44,740
hosts the largest county fair in North America, each year

11
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:46,760
attracting over 1 million visitors.

12
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:51,760
No visit is complete without paying homage

13
00:00:51,860 --> 00:00:56,260
to the fair's traditional mascot, the pig.

14
00:00:56,360 --> 00:00:59,200
The show pig competition is serious business.

15
00:00:59,201 --> 00:01:05,800
business, each competitor trying to produce the ultimate physical specimen.

16
00:01:05,801 --> 00:01:09,440
Behind every champion pig is a doting mother.

17
00:01:09,441 --> 00:01:14,120
This sow is a wonderful example of what we hoped that a production sow would look like.

18
00:01:14,121 --> 00:01:20,160
She's 10 years old, she has a phenomenal udder line on her still, she's produced 18 litters

19
00:01:20,161 --> 00:01:21,160
of pigs.

20
00:01:21,161 --> 00:01:26,580
The sow is the most prolific reproducing animal on the farm.

21
00:01:26,581 --> 00:01:28,420
between 6 and 12 pigs per litter,

22
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:30,960
with the capability of producing

23
00:01:30,961 --> 00:01:34,180
more than 250 piglets over her lifetime.

24
00:01:34,620 --> 00:01:37,100
She will spend two-thirds of her life pregnant.

25
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:40,400
What you're seeing is dual activity.

26
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:43,880
Mom is doing what she does best,

27
00:01:44,060 --> 00:01:45,540
and that's sleeping.

28
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:48,240
And the pigs are doing what they're doing best,

29
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:49,020
and that's eating.

30
00:01:49,220 --> 00:01:51,740
When they're little, within the first,

31
00:01:52,060 --> 00:01:54,860
oh, I'd say 12, 24 hours,

32
00:01:54,861 --> 00:01:58,940
they actually lock onto one particular teat that is theirs,

33
00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:01,480
and they go to it each time.

34
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:04,400
They'll fight to the death to keep that teat.

35
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:09,160
And usually what happens is the bigger, stronger babies that are born

36
00:02:09,161 --> 00:02:15,060
root the smaller, weaker babies to the teats that don't give as much milk.

37
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:21,400
The runts sometimes succumb to the laws of natural selection and perish.

38
00:02:21,401 --> 00:02:24,200
Even so, a sow is fiercely protected

39
00:02:24,201 --> 00:02:27,140
and will fight to the death to keep her piglets safe.

40
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:28,940
Watch her, watch her, watch her.

41
00:02:30,140 --> 00:02:33,240
They're the most dangerous animal on the farm.

42
00:02:33,540 --> 00:02:36,700
It's a sow with baby pigs, more so than a bull.

43
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:40,980
They're strong in the front shoulders and the neck and the head,

44
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:43,240
comparable to a grizzly bear.

45
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:46,600
And when they bite, they don't bite you like a dog,

46
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:48,400
sink their fangs in and turn loose.

47
00:02:48,401 --> 00:02:52,020
They bite and rip a piece of meat off of you.

48
00:02:52,460 --> 00:02:57,740
So a hog bite is extremely painful, really, really serious.

49
00:02:58,420 --> 00:03:03,560
A pig's powerful jaws contain 44 enamel-coated incisors, canines, and molars,

50
00:03:04,260 --> 00:03:06,160
12 more teeth than an adult human.

51
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:10,900
The four canines, or tusks, grow naturally,

52
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:14,460
but are often clipped off from domestic pigs days after birth

53
00:03:14,461 --> 00:03:16,080
to prevent injury to other animals.

54
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:24,200
Though well equipped to bite, pigs are best known for their legendary snout.

55
00:03:24,201 --> 00:03:30,280
So sensitive, they are often used to root out the highly prized edible fungi called truffles.

56
00:03:30,281 --> 00:03:36,740
But most truffle hunters prefer trained dogs instead of pigs, who tend to eat the precious

57
00:03:36,741 --> 00:03:38,960
treasures.

58
00:03:38,961 --> 00:03:42,880
Some call it a greedy appetite, and some call it smart.

59
00:03:42,881 --> 00:03:54,120
If you're around them, you will soon figure out that these animals have a thinking ability similar to what people understand in the dog.

60
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:58,560
They can figure a way to tear up a fence, go through a hole.

61
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:01,220
Terrific time keepers.

62
00:04:02,260 --> 00:04:06,080
Some have equated their intelligence to that of an average human three-year-old.

63
00:04:06,081 --> 00:04:14,820
But as much as we celebrate the pig's intelligence and charisma, we mainly enjoy eating him.

64
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:20,780
Whether you call him a pig or a hog, his flesh is the world's most widely consumed meat.

65
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:26,360
In the United States, it ranks third behind beef and chicken,

66
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:30,780
with the average American pigging out on 51 pounds of pork annually.

67
00:04:30,781 --> 00:04:36,780
Today's hog is a veritable butcher shop of edible meats.

68
00:04:36,781 --> 00:04:40,780
The most popular products come from four distinct quarters.

69
00:04:40,781 --> 00:04:42,780
The leg yields ham.

70
00:04:42,781 --> 00:04:45,780
The shoulder, picnic ham and roasts.

71
00:04:45,781 --> 00:04:48,780
The belly, bacon and spare ribs.

72
00:04:48,781 --> 00:04:53,780
And the loin produces tenderloins, chops and back ribs.

73
00:04:53,781 --> 00:04:55,780
Consider bacon.

74
00:04:55,781 --> 00:04:59,780
This Tyson facility processes 600,000 pounds of pork bellies

75
00:04:59,781 --> 00:05:02,780
in a single day, trimming, shaping, and slicing

76
00:05:02,781 --> 00:05:05,780
at a furious pace.

77
00:05:05,781 --> 00:05:08,780
The bellies are first skinned and then injected

78
00:05:08,781 --> 00:05:12,780
with a curing solution that helps preserve and flavor the pork.

79
00:05:12,781 --> 00:05:17,780
This is a critical stage in the process of making bacon.

80
00:05:17,781 --> 00:05:20,780
This is where the curing ingredients are injected

81
00:05:20,781 --> 00:05:22,780
into the pork belly.

82
00:05:22,781 --> 00:05:26,780
Inside this injector, there are 168 needles

83
00:05:26,781 --> 00:05:30,620
that are injecting a solution of water, salt, sugar,

84
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:32,920
sodium phosphate, sodium erythorbate,

85
00:05:33,020 --> 00:05:37,220
and sodium nitrite into the belly.

86
00:05:37,320 --> 00:05:41,820
A quick seasoning stop in a 135-degree thermal oven,

87
00:05:41,920 --> 00:05:45,940
followed by a pressing to form rectangular blocks.

88
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:49,740
And this bacon is ready for slicing.

89
00:05:49,840 --> 00:05:52,580
Little time is wasted as the slicing blade cuts the bellies

90
00:05:52,680 --> 00:05:55,680
at 1,500 revolutions per minute.

91
00:05:55,681 --> 00:06:00,920
piecing out exact one-pound units that are vacuum-packed and ready for sale.

92
00:06:03,260 --> 00:06:05,840
Tyson even caters to bacon lovers on the go.

93
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:08,400
Pre-cooked bacon.

94
00:06:10,940 --> 00:06:13,900
After a high-speed slicer makes quick work of the bellies,

95
00:06:14,700 --> 00:06:16,780
the bacon slices get zapped to perfection.

96
00:06:18,380 --> 00:06:23,740
This monster microwave boasts more heating power than 2,500 home microwave ovens.

97
00:06:25,680 --> 00:06:28,120
From the time the pork bellies were put in this slicer,

98
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:30,580
cooked, chilled, and packaged,

99
00:06:31,020 --> 00:06:32,280
less than two minutes later,

100
00:06:32,320 --> 00:06:34,700
we got a finished product ready to eat.

101
00:06:36,120 --> 00:06:37,680
If bacon is breakfast,

102
00:06:38,300 --> 00:06:40,880
a ham sandwich must be lunch.

103
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:45,500
Burger's Smokehouse in California, Missouri,

104
00:06:46,020 --> 00:06:47,880
has a busy mail-order business

105
00:06:47,881 --> 00:06:49,680
that churns out 12 million pounds

106
00:06:49,681 --> 00:06:51,540
of specialty ham products every year.

107
00:06:51,541 --> 00:06:59,540
Before you can make a ham sandwich, every whole leg brimal cut must be split and deboned.

108
00:06:59,541 --> 00:07:06,540
From here, each ham takes one of two curing directions, city or country.

109
00:07:06,541 --> 00:07:11,540
City hams are injected with a brine mixture for instant wet curing,

110
00:07:11,541 --> 00:07:14,540
the most popular way of processing ham.

111
00:07:14,541 --> 00:07:20,540
Country hams are dry cured with salt and then hung up in bags the old-fashioned way for months of curing.

112
00:07:20,541 --> 00:07:23,420
for months of curing.

113
00:07:23,421 --> 00:07:26,360
Finally, the hams meet the knife.

114
00:07:26,361 --> 00:07:28,100
What we're doing is removing the femur bone

115
00:07:28,101 --> 00:07:29,360
out of the center chunk of the ham.

116
00:07:29,361 --> 00:07:32,840
The femur bone runs down the middle of the center.

117
00:07:32,841 --> 00:07:37,220
We take our knife and we run it around the bone

118
00:07:37,221 --> 00:07:41,400
so that we can release the bone from the rest of the ham.

119
00:07:41,401 --> 00:07:46,020
Now I'm skinning the ham, removing the waste.

120
00:07:46,021 --> 00:07:49,340
So when the customer gets this package of ham home,

121
00:07:49,341 --> 00:07:51,740
There's very little waste.

122
00:07:51,741 --> 00:07:54,340
In the portion control room, the cutting

123
00:07:54,341 --> 00:07:56,480
gets fast and high tech.

124
00:07:56,481 --> 00:08:00,720
We're cutting dry-cure country hams on bandsaws today.

125
00:08:00,721 --> 00:08:02,080
The work that we're doing in this room

126
00:08:02,081 --> 00:08:04,860
is primarily for the restaurant division.

127
00:08:04,861 --> 00:08:09,920
The sawers can each cut approximately 150 hams a day.

128
00:08:09,921 --> 00:08:12,740
We then take the center slices that come off this product,

129
00:08:12,741 --> 00:08:15,740
and then they're taken to a water knife.

130
00:08:15,741 --> 00:08:17,400
The computer-controlled water knife

131
00:08:17,401 --> 00:08:21,140
applies the final trim to approximately 10,000 pounds

132
00:08:21,141 --> 00:08:22,480
of ham steaks per day.

133
00:08:23,780 --> 00:08:26,540
Far more efficient than a small army of human trimmers.

134
00:08:30,080 --> 00:08:32,220
This is the inside of the water knife.

135
00:08:32,221 --> 00:08:34,120
The computer will read it.

136
00:08:34,121 --> 00:08:38,580
Then it utilizes two water jets working concurrently

137
00:08:38,581 --> 00:08:40,620
to cut the product into spec.

138
00:08:40,621 --> 00:08:43,120
It reads both height, weight, density

139
00:08:43,121 --> 00:08:45,000
to make those calculations to be sure

140
00:08:45,001 --> 00:08:47,840
that the end product is the right weight and spec.

141
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:53,120
The Jets cut with 50,000 pounds per square inch of pressure

142
00:08:53,121 --> 00:08:54,820
to trim and size the stakes.

143
00:08:55,720 --> 00:08:58,600
A video camera identifies and rejects slices

144
00:08:58,601 --> 00:09:00,320
that fall outside of specifications.

145
00:09:02,520 --> 00:09:06,260
For many ham addicts, single slices simply won't do.

146
00:09:06,900 --> 00:09:10,940
The spiral cutting process was developed a number of years ago,

147
00:09:10,941 --> 00:09:13,680
And it basically allows the consumer

148
00:09:13,780 --> 00:09:17,620
to enjoy the flavor that comes from cooking the product

149
00:09:17,720 --> 00:09:21,960
with the bone in, but with the convenience of having it sliced.

150
00:09:22,060 --> 00:09:24,920
The finishing touch is a sugary glaze,

151
00:09:25,020 --> 00:09:28,860
caramelized by hand at 500 degrees.

152
00:09:28,960 --> 00:09:32,360
Burgers, ham, and bacon products are packaged for sale,

153
00:09:32,460 --> 00:09:33,960
but nothing is wasted.

154
00:09:34,060 --> 00:09:37,700
What we have here are ham hocks that have just been cooked

155
00:09:37,701 --> 00:09:39,960
over our slow period and cooked in water.

156
00:09:41,100 --> 00:09:44,840
We're now separating from the bone and the fat.

157
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:49,060
We're saving the meat portion of the product.

158
00:09:49,260 --> 00:09:51,420
It's a process that has to be done by hand.

159
00:09:51,860 --> 00:09:54,400
You simply, no machine will do this procedure.

160
00:09:55,940 --> 00:09:57,900
The ham hocks are mixed with a broth

161
00:09:57,901 --> 00:10:02,240
and will eventually wind up as a seasoning for turnip greens.

162
00:10:04,840 --> 00:10:07,080
These tasty hams bear little resemblance

163
00:10:07,081 --> 00:10:08,940
to those of the pigs' wild ancestors.

164
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:13,140
The earliest fossilized remains found in Africa

165
00:10:13,141 --> 00:10:15,760
date back 10 million years.

166
00:10:17,060 --> 00:10:21,040
In 1539, pigs first arrived on the American mainland

167
00:10:21,041 --> 00:10:23,700
when Spanish explorer Hernando de Soto

168
00:10:23,701 --> 00:10:26,200
brought a small domestic herd to Tampa Bay, Florida.

169
00:10:28,160 --> 00:10:30,900
The pigs quickly became an important food source

170
00:10:30,901 --> 00:10:32,840
for colonists and Native Americans alike.

171
00:10:33,660 --> 00:10:35,920
Some pigs were kept for food.

172
00:10:35,921 --> 00:10:39,100
Others were allowed to breed, and some broke loose.

173
00:10:39,360 --> 00:10:41,200
From those pigs that broke loose,

174
00:10:41,340 --> 00:10:44,160
that actually much of the pig was able to spread then

175
00:10:44,161 --> 00:10:48,920
throughout the colonies and into much of what was North America at the time.

176
00:10:49,860 --> 00:10:54,300
Roaming bands of feral hogs often used their powerful snouts to uprood crops.

177
00:10:55,880 --> 00:10:59,880
On Manhattan Island, colonial farmers built a long, solid wall

178
00:10:59,881 --> 00:11:01,880
to keep out rampaging wild pigs.

179
00:11:01,881 --> 00:11:06,040
This area is today known as Wall Street.

180
00:11:07,360 --> 00:11:11,480
By the mid-1800s, Cincinnati became a central location

181
00:11:11,481 --> 00:11:13,480
for farmers delivering pigs for slaughter

182
00:11:13,481 --> 00:11:16,820
and earned the nickname Porkopolis.

183
00:11:17,340 --> 00:11:19,920
When refrigerated rail cars came along,

184
00:11:20,240 --> 00:11:24,160
it changed the whole dynamic again of how you processed pigs

185
00:11:24,161 --> 00:11:25,560
and how far they could be shipped.

186
00:11:26,860 --> 00:11:30,480
Refrigeration and mechanization revolutionized the packing houses.

187
00:11:30,481 --> 00:11:37,480
The revolution swept aside the family-owned pig farm and crowned the corporate farm king

188
00:11:37,481 --> 00:11:40,480
in the 1990s.

189
00:11:40,481 --> 00:11:47,480
The 21st century pig farm trades the old world barnyard for high-tech husbandry.

190
00:11:47,481 --> 00:11:51,480
This Iowa Select Farms facility is all about safety.

191
00:11:51,481 --> 00:11:58,480
For the pigs, that means a mandatory shower and clean clothes for the workers and our camera crew.

192
00:11:58,481 --> 00:12:05,480
Biosecurity here at this farm is very important for our company to keep outside diseases from coming into our herd.

193
00:12:05,481 --> 00:12:11,480
If we keep our pigs healthy, it increases our profitability and we have a better product for the consumer.

194
00:12:15,480 --> 00:12:22,480
As we enter the facility, this is a typical wean to finish facility for our company.

195
00:12:22,481 --> 00:12:25,480
Pigs come here at about 21 days of age.

196
00:12:25,481 --> 00:12:29,020
They will stay here until they're market ready, which

197
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:31,320
is about 260 pounds.

198
00:12:31,420 --> 00:12:34,980
Once they reach that point, then they're shipped to a packer

199
00:12:35,080 --> 00:12:36,920
to be processed.

200
00:12:37,020 --> 00:12:41,320
The roll call in this facility is 2,500 pigs.

201
00:12:41,420 --> 00:12:44,240
To keep the peace, they're segregated into pens

202
00:12:44,340 --> 00:12:47,940
to reduce competition for food.

203
00:12:48,040 --> 00:12:51,540
Outside, a pair of 15-ton bulk bins

204
00:12:51,541 --> 00:12:54,080
supply a steady stream of pellets to each pen.

205
00:12:55,940 --> 00:12:58,760
The pigs manipulate a circular trigger to release the food,

206
00:12:59,500 --> 00:13:03,000
a precise mixture of corn, soybeans, amino acids,

207
00:13:03,160 --> 00:13:04,360
and other minerals.

208
00:13:06,440 --> 00:13:10,020
Unlike past decades when pigs were sometimes fed meat scraps,

209
00:13:10,760 --> 00:13:13,260
the grain-based diet of today's pig is a big reason

210
00:13:13,261 --> 00:13:15,760
why the parasitic disease trichinosis

211
00:13:15,761 --> 00:13:17,740
occurs very rarely in pork.

212
00:13:17,741 --> 00:13:21,740
But what goes in must come out.

213
00:13:21,741 --> 00:13:26,740
And what comes out has been plaguing farmers and the environment for decades.

214
00:13:26,741 --> 00:13:33,740
The way this facility is designed is it's a total slatted, deep pit facility.

215
00:13:33,741 --> 00:13:36,740
So the manure storage is underneath the facility.

216
00:13:36,741 --> 00:13:40,740
There's an eight-foot deep pit that holds the liquid manure.

217
00:13:40,741 --> 00:13:44,740
The manure is able to pass through the cracks in the floor.

218
00:13:44,741 --> 00:13:49,740
It's a slatted floor, so the pigs have a relatively clean environment.

219
00:13:49,741 --> 00:13:55,740
The noxious pool of waste emits hydrogen sulfide, a toxic gas.

220
00:13:55,741 --> 00:14:00,740
Just two or three breaths of it at high concentration can kill you.

221
00:14:00,741 --> 00:14:05,740
Approximately once a year, pumps remove the slurry from the subterranean tank,

222
00:14:05,741 --> 00:14:10,740
and farmers inject it into their fields as fertilizer.

223
00:14:10,741 --> 00:14:13,240
But the pigs aren't too concerned about waste.

224
00:14:13,340 --> 00:14:16,080
High heat is their adversary.

225
00:14:16,180 --> 00:14:19,580
Pigs don't have sweat glands and are at risk on hot days.

226
00:14:19,680 --> 00:14:22,420
We like to keep our pigs, this size of pig,

227
00:14:22,520 --> 00:14:25,760
about 74 degrees.

228
00:14:25,860 --> 00:14:28,260
And we will slowly move that temperature down

229
00:14:28,360 --> 00:14:29,860
as the pigs get bigger.

230
00:14:29,960 --> 00:14:31,660
We want to keep the pig comfortable,

231
00:14:31,760 --> 00:14:34,400
make him produce as many pounds of pork as possible.

232
00:14:36,600 --> 00:14:38,360
In this barn, temperature control

233
00:14:38,460 --> 00:14:40,100
is an exact science.

234
00:14:40,101 --> 00:14:43,100
When the temperature exceeds 74 degrees,

235
00:14:43,101 --> 00:14:46,100
a sensor automatically lowers a plastic curtain

236
00:14:46,101 --> 00:14:49,100
to allow in the cooling breeze.

237
00:14:49,101 --> 00:14:52,100
To manage clean air flow, eight external fans

238
00:14:52,101 --> 00:14:55,100
pulled the subterranean manure gases out of the barn.

239
00:14:57,100 --> 00:15:00,100
The resulting vacuum pulls in fresh air from the attic

240
00:15:00,101 --> 00:15:03,100
through vents in a continuous cycle.

241
00:15:03,101 --> 00:15:05,100
The barn can even call the farmer

242
00:15:05,101 --> 00:15:08,100
if any of the environmental controls malfunction.

243
00:15:08,101 --> 00:15:09,900
function.

244
00:15:09,901 --> 00:15:13,320
While modern farmers like Iowa Select embrace new technology

245
00:15:13,321 --> 00:15:15,660
to raise a healthy pig, scientists

246
00:15:15,661 --> 00:15:18,420
are taking this a step further.

247
00:15:18,421 --> 00:15:21,500
Heart-healthy pork chops from a DNA-altered pig, anyone?

248
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,900
The human population of North Carolina's Duplin County

249
00:15:26,901 --> 00:15:29,240
is near 50,000.

250
00:15:29,241 --> 00:15:32,240
The pig population is near 2 million,

251
00:15:32,241 --> 00:15:36,420
making it one of the most populous pig counties in America.

252
00:15:36,421 --> 00:15:39,460
The pig will return on Modern Marvels.

253
00:15:42,960 --> 00:15:44,720
Hey, we are the Barnyard Racers,

254
00:15:44,800 --> 00:15:46,440
and we're going to have some big racing fun

255
00:15:46,441 --> 00:15:47,600
for you this afternoon.

256
00:15:47,940 --> 00:15:49,040
Hey, we're glad you're here.

257
00:15:50,140 --> 00:15:52,640
The tension builds as the contestants

258
00:15:52,641 --> 00:15:53,800
are loaded into the gate.

259
00:15:54,560 --> 00:15:56,880
These racing pigs may lack focus,

260
00:15:57,380 --> 00:15:59,100
but their handlers know how to motivate.

261
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,040
They use milk and Oreo cookies.

262
00:16:02,360 --> 00:16:04,180
It's our favorite thing in the whole world.

263
00:16:04,181 --> 00:16:09,000
They may lack the supple grace of their barnyard sidekick, the horse,

264
00:16:09,640 --> 00:16:12,040
but some pigs can run a seven-minute mile.

265
00:16:13,260 --> 00:16:15,120
Their speed is the product of breeding

266
00:16:15,121 --> 00:16:19,260
because the modern pig is bred for a higher percentage of lean meat to fat.

267
00:16:20,320 --> 00:16:24,460
In past centuries, a lean hog was the last thing anyone wanted.

268
00:16:25,940 --> 00:16:29,660
Few pigs packed on the pounds like this 1,000-pound porker,

269
00:16:30,300 --> 00:16:32,960
but one Tennessee hog named Big Bill

270
00:16:32,961 --> 00:16:37,540
set the world record at more than 2,500 pounds.

271
00:16:38,640 --> 00:16:41,020
Regrettably, no pictures of Bill exist today.

272
00:16:41,380 --> 00:16:45,040
For a farmer here 150 years ago, that fat was important as lard.

273
00:16:45,180 --> 00:16:46,380
They're cooking with that lard.

274
00:16:46,500 --> 00:16:47,700
They're burning lard in lamps.

275
00:16:47,780 --> 00:16:49,080
They're making soap out of it.

276
00:16:49,380 --> 00:16:52,360
So some could say that the fat was as important as the meat to the hog.

277
00:16:52,361 --> 00:16:58,100
And so the use of the pig from 1880 to about 1940 or so

278
00:16:58,101 --> 00:17:01,140
was effectively focused on production of lard.

279
00:17:02,040 --> 00:17:05,380
Lard also played a surprisingly key role in modern warfare.

280
00:17:06,420 --> 00:17:08,300
During World War I and World War II,

281
00:17:08,920 --> 00:17:13,140
lard was used as an essential ingredient in creating nitroglycerin,

282
00:17:13,820 --> 00:17:16,940
which is a base ingredient in munitions and in explosives.

283
00:17:17,780 --> 00:17:21,600
And so lard effectively was a piece of winning the war.

284
00:17:22,200 --> 00:17:27,280
By the 1950s and 60s, lard was widely replaced by vegetable oils

285
00:17:27,281 --> 00:17:30,120
as consumers began to avoid fattening foods.

286
00:17:31,140 --> 00:17:37,580
Today's market hog has evolved from yesterday's round, fatty lard factory with nearly three

287
00:17:37,581 --> 00:17:44,800
inches of back fat to a leaner, more muscular creature with less than one inch of back fat.

288
00:17:44,801 --> 00:17:49,640
The loin and belly are now much longer, ideal for producing more pounds of bacon and pork

289
00:17:49,641 --> 00:17:50,640
chops.

290
00:17:50,641 --> 00:17:54,780
We've also reduced the amount of calories and cholesterol in that animal.

291
00:17:54,781 --> 00:18:00,600
But unfortunately the reputation of being a lard type animal, a fatty animal, stayed

292
00:18:00,601 --> 00:18:02,060
with a pig for a long time.

293
00:18:02,640 --> 00:18:06,140
So we began the Other White Meat campaign in 1986.

294
00:18:06,820 --> 00:18:08,980
Make it with the Other White Meat instead.

295
00:18:09,360 --> 00:18:12,260
The most delicious things happen when you cut into pork.

296
00:18:12,820 --> 00:18:14,280
The Other White Meat.

297
00:18:16,080 --> 00:18:19,320
Pork, like chicken, contains a low count of myoglobin,

298
00:18:20,140 --> 00:18:22,500
a protein that adds darker color and muscle tissue.

299
00:18:24,360 --> 00:18:27,080
The Other White Meat advertising campaign

300
00:18:27,081 --> 00:18:30,160
boosted pork demand by 15% in the United States.

301
00:18:30,600 --> 00:18:38,600
The farmers help meet that demand with artificial insemination, or AI.

302
00:18:38,601 --> 00:18:41,800
AI increases reproductive frequency.

303
00:18:41,801 --> 00:18:46,720
More importantly, it's a perfect opportunity for pig breeders to select certain traits

304
00:18:46,721 --> 00:18:50,360
to pass along to offspring.

305
00:18:50,361 --> 00:18:56,320
International Boar Semen in Eldora, Iowa is working to sire the finest pigs on the planet.

306
00:18:56,321 --> 00:19:02,640
Here the genesis of our pork chops, hams, and BLTs is collected in small tubes.

307
00:19:02,641 --> 00:19:05,280
Artificial insemination has many benefits.

308
00:19:05,281 --> 00:19:09,760
The first is that from a cost factor we can take and spread one boar out through very

309
00:19:09,761 --> 00:19:13,920
many more matings and over much more of a population to spread his genetics throughout

310
00:19:13,921 --> 00:19:14,920
that population.

311
00:19:14,921 --> 00:19:21,120
It also allows greater biosecurity so you have less transportation of diseases from

312
00:19:21,121 --> 00:19:23,440
the boars to the sows.

313
00:19:23,441 --> 00:19:30,160
The IBS boars live the stud lifestyle in an isolated biosecure facility.

314
00:19:30,161 --> 00:19:35,840
Because they will sire virtually thousands of pigs, germ control is vital.

315
00:19:35,841 --> 00:19:40,660
Access for the Modern Marvels camera crew is limited to a small viewing window.

316
00:19:40,661 --> 00:19:46,000
It may not look like a female pig to you, but this sow dummy is the only help a boar

317
00:19:46,001 --> 00:19:47,500
needs to get in the mood.

318
00:19:47,501 --> 00:19:53,240
We'll use a gloved hand to make sure that it's very sanitary to collect the boar he's

319
00:19:53,241 --> 00:19:57,720
extended out and collected and then the semen is kept in an incubator to maintain temperature.

320
00:19:57,721 --> 00:20:00,200
Temperature is very crucial at this point.

321
00:20:00,201 --> 00:20:04,280
The natural semen has a short lifespan of several hours.

322
00:20:06,040 --> 00:20:11,000
But the lab technicians know how to extend that and they have to be sure the semen is vital.

323
00:20:12,600 --> 00:20:16,439
We're going to get a what we call a spec reading.

324
00:20:16,440 --> 00:20:20,200
That's going to help us determine the density of

325
00:20:20,201 --> 00:20:28,464
of the semen. We took a sample of the semen and we put it on the slide here.

326
00:20:28,465 --> 00:20:29,660
We're going

327
00:20:29,661 --> 00:20:37,276
to look for abnormalities and motility. As you can see that sample looks very good.

328
00:20:37,277 --> 00:20:37,977
We

329
00:20:37,978 --> 00:20:45,440
don't see a lot of abnormalities in there. Once viability is confirmed, the semen is

330
00:20:45,441 --> 00:20:50,100
added to a sugar-based extender solution that prolongs its integrity for several days

331
00:20:50,101 --> 00:20:51,796
during shipping.

332
00:20:51,797 --> 00:20:59,640
Artificial insemination helps build a superior pig, but researchers at the

333
00:20:59,641 --> 00:21:05,360
Center for Integrated Animal Genomics at Iowa State University want to fine-tune him even further.

334
00:21:06,460 --> 00:21:11,060
They're using genetic sequencing in pursuit of the ultimate super swine.

335
00:21:11,320 --> 00:21:16,620
The pig is what we might call a plastic animal. It's easily changed by genetics.

336
00:21:16,621 --> 00:21:18,360
And this isn't by gene modification.

337
00:21:18,460 --> 00:21:21,560
This is just by selection.

338
00:21:21,660 --> 00:21:24,560
Scientists decipher the pig's genetic code

339
00:21:24,660 --> 00:21:28,260
through a complex gene sequencing process.

340
00:21:28,360 --> 00:21:32,060
We'll open up a whole new toolbox, if you like,

341
00:21:32,160 --> 00:21:35,580
of the 25,000 odd genes that the pig has.

342
00:21:35,680 --> 00:21:38,280
And we'll start to learn what they each individually do

343
00:21:38,380 --> 00:21:41,320
and what they do together to make a better pig.

344
00:21:41,420 --> 00:21:45,920
We've discovered genes that improve litter size by about 10%.

345
00:21:45,921 --> 00:21:50,620
We found genes that affect growth rate and reduce manure output.

346
00:21:50,940 --> 00:21:53,580
We found genes that increase the amount of lean,

347
00:21:54,080 --> 00:21:57,480
and more recently we've been working on genes that affect sow longevity.

348
00:21:58,760 --> 00:22:03,560
Once these genes are isolated, researchers can introduce changes to the gene pool

349
00:22:03,561 --> 00:22:07,820
and help pork producers cut costs and serve up a better pig.

350
00:22:10,040 --> 00:22:12,060
This pig is a clone,

351
00:22:12,061 --> 00:22:16,300
and he's one of several special pigs at the University of Missouri

352
00:22:16,301 --> 00:22:18,560
that could one day make us healthier.

353
00:22:20,400 --> 00:22:25,420
These are the only pigs in the world that contain high levels of omega-3 fatty acids.

354
00:22:26,820 --> 00:22:31,260
Omega-3s, so-called good fats, that convert to good cholesterol,

355
00:22:32,020 --> 00:22:34,340
are essential to human brain and heart function.

356
00:22:35,300 --> 00:22:37,820
But they cannot be manufactured by the human body.

357
00:22:37,821 --> 00:22:43,280
They are found only in certain plants and fish, like wild salmon and tuna.

358
00:22:44,600 --> 00:22:54,800
To produce a high-level omega-3 pig, researchers inserted a foreign cell containing a gene called FAT1 into fetal pig cells that were then cloned.

359
00:22:56,060 --> 00:23:04,900
The pig on a treadmill stress test is one way to compare the cardiovascular system of pigs with high levels of omega-3s to their counterparts with low levels.

360
00:23:04,901 --> 00:23:10,900
The results will tell science much about how omega-3s affect the human heart.

361
00:23:10,901 --> 00:23:19,900
I think what this will provide us is just another tool to really begin to understand the effects of omega-3 fatty acids on the cardiovascular system,

362
00:23:19,901 --> 00:23:23,900
the immune system, the reproductive system, the prevention of cancer.

363
00:23:23,901 --> 00:23:31,900
The medical benefits from these heart-healthy pigs are potentially limitless, but pork producers may see dollar signs.

364
00:23:31,901 --> 00:23:37,440
I look forward in the future sometime when FDA would approve something like this to enter the food chain

365
00:23:37,441 --> 00:23:44,780
to be able to have pork that has high levels of omega-3 fatty acids that would be very healthy to consume.

366
00:23:45,540 --> 00:23:49,380
From a walking lard factory to transgenic super swine,

367
00:23:49,960 --> 00:23:53,380
the pig has unknowingly responded to our attempts to rewire him.

368
00:23:54,660 --> 00:24:00,880
But sometimes the key to producing the finest tasting pigs on earth is a bag of acorns.

369
00:24:01,900 --> 00:24:09,580
During the War of 1812, a New York packer named Uncle Sam Wilson shipped several hundred

370
00:24:09,581 --> 00:24:12,640
barrels of salt pork to American troops.

371
00:24:12,641 --> 00:24:15,420
Each barrel was stamped U.S.

372
00:24:15,421 --> 00:24:20,160
This was how Uncle Sam came to represent the United States government.

373
00:24:20,161 --> 00:24:26,480
The pig will return on Modern Marvels.

374
00:24:26,481 --> 00:24:30,900
In South Carolina, a barbecue competition is right up there with the Super Bowl.

375
00:24:30,901 --> 00:24:35,040
And it's usually about one thing, slow-cooked whole hog.

376
00:24:35,700 --> 00:24:39,620
This hog will cook for approximately 20 hours at 200 degrees.

377
00:24:39,720 --> 00:24:43,460
We like to stay low and slow because we want the meat texture to stay together.

378
00:24:43,760 --> 00:24:45,380
We want the pork flavor to come out.

379
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:51,220
Barbecuing low and slow will transform the toughest meat into a tender feast.

380
00:24:51,980 --> 00:24:54,220
But most of us don't go whole hog.

381
00:24:55,080 --> 00:24:57,960
Our pork comes cut to size and shrink-wrapped.

382
00:24:57,961 --> 00:25:03,840
But as anyone who's tasted a tough chop knows, not all pork is created equal.

383
00:25:03,841 --> 00:25:08,000
I think consumers are becoming much more sensitive to food quality.

384
00:25:08,001 --> 00:25:11,000
Nobody likes to buy something that's not going to taste good.

385
00:25:11,001 --> 00:25:14,000
To be certain their meat is in peak condition,

386
00:25:14,001 --> 00:25:20,920
many producers submit samples for a pork checkup at Iowa State University's Sensory Evaluation Lab.

387
00:25:20,921 --> 00:25:24,920
The lab runs the raw pork through every conceivable test.

388
00:25:24,921 --> 00:25:30,320
Still, the ultimate judge is often the human palate.

389
00:25:30,321 --> 00:25:36,920
We do train sensory panels with tenderness, juiciness and flavor to actually train individuals,

390
00:25:36,921 --> 00:25:43,920
use them as instruments to determine if a producer changes genetics, we'll be able to tell them how it affects the quality.

391
00:25:43,921 --> 00:25:49,920
In recent years, higher quality pork is not what we once thought it was.

392
00:25:49,921 --> 00:25:52,560
Color is another very important factor.

393
00:25:52,561 --> 00:25:57,640
I know we've talked about the other white meat for quite some time in relationship to nutrition,

394
00:25:58,340 --> 00:26:01,600
but we find that slightly darker pork is actually of higher quality.

395
00:26:02,220 --> 00:26:08,220
Many experts believe that darker colored, better tasting pork is directly related to higher fat content.

396
00:26:09,980 --> 00:26:15,780
So a few maverick farmers are seeking to return the pig to its more flavorful, fatty past.

397
00:26:15,781 --> 00:26:23,880
At Becker Lane, a sixth generation pig farm in Dyersville, Iowa, this throwback approach

398
00:26:23,881 --> 00:26:29,340
is called organic pig farming, a way of life that might cause the pigs at a large corporate

399
00:26:29,341 --> 00:26:33,060
run farm to turn green with envy.

400
00:26:33,061 --> 00:26:39,060
Our emphasis here is providing quality of life for the pig and also for the farmers that

401
00:26:39,061 --> 00:26:40,620
are involved in the production.

402
00:26:40,621 --> 00:26:43,420
So we provide the pig with a lot more space.

403
00:26:43,421 --> 00:26:49,180
We believe that the pigs, when they are given so much space to move around and eat organic

404
00:26:49,181 --> 00:26:54,440
grain that will show up and express itself in the texture of the meat.

405
00:26:54,441 --> 00:26:59,880
The Becker Lane pigs begin their free-range life in a farrowing field, a place where sows

406
00:26:59,881 --> 00:27:02,840
give birth and nurture their piglets.

407
00:27:02,841 --> 00:27:06,900
So every sow has her own space.

408
00:27:06,901 --> 00:27:14,000
So she can have her own food and her own house to live in, which we call farrowing hut.

409
00:27:14,001 --> 00:27:19,300
She's free to go in there and give birth to her piglets and come out here and drink from

410
00:27:19,301 --> 00:27:20,300
this water system.

411
00:27:20,301 --> 00:27:23,100
If she wants, she can make a mud hole.

412
00:27:23,101 --> 00:27:25,620
So they have lots of options.

413
00:27:25,621 --> 00:27:28,780
They can live a normal pig life.

414
00:27:28,781 --> 00:27:32,860
Thanks to their unaltered genetics, the pigs retain extra fat layers.

415
00:27:32,861 --> 00:27:37,420
the help of insulated farrowing huts they can withstand the harshest midwest winters

416
00:27:39,740 --> 00:27:44,220
when the piglets reach seven weeks they move to some european style accommodations

417
00:27:44,780 --> 00:27:49,100
the hoop barn so this is a hoop barn and

418
00:27:53,420 --> 00:27:59,740
inside you see we have some pigs and a hoop barn is any any building that we

419
00:27:59,741 --> 00:28:06,880
is built with these metal poles to create a rafter system and over the metal poles we put

420
00:28:06,881 --> 00:28:11,620
some kind of fabric in this case plastic we're trying to manipulate the micro climate here so

421
00:28:11,621 --> 00:28:19,900
the pigs can be comfortable all the time and that reduces stress so how do these pigs stay warm when

422
00:28:19,901 --> 00:28:27,980
it's freezing it's all in the straw originally invented in Sweden a hoop barn uses a two foot

423
00:28:27,981 --> 00:28:31,460
deep layer of straw that absorbs and decomposes the pig's

424
00:28:31,461 --> 00:28:34,860
manure, generating heat.

425
00:28:34,861 --> 00:28:39,920
The mixture is replaced regularly and composted on the farm.

426
00:28:39,921 --> 00:28:43,700
This eco-recycling is responsible farm management,

427
00:28:43,701 --> 00:28:47,600
but it's the pedigreed pigs that bring home the bacon.

428
00:28:47,601 --> 00:28:51,540
Becker Lane specializes in purebred heritage breeds,

429
00:28:51,541 --> 00:28:55,080
such as the jet black Berkshire hog.

430
00:28:55,081 --> 00:28:57,340
The Berkshires fame began in the 17th century,

431
00:28:57,700 --> 00:29:01,960
when Oliver Cromwell's army brought a small herd back to the English royal family.

432
00:29:03,620 --> 00:29:06,580
The king later gave a gift of Berkshire hogs to the Japanese.

433
00:29:08,520 --> 00:29:13,000
Today, it's reverently known in Japan as kurabuta, or black pig.

434
00:29:13,480 --> 00:29:18,140
And the vast majority of American-raised Berkshire pork is exported to Japan.

435
00:29:19,540 --> 00:29:23,420
In the United States, it's now a trendy main course in upscale restaurants.

436
00:29:23,421 --> 00:29:28,420
The pure Berkshire pork, or kurabuta, is definitely darker.

437
00:29:28,421 --> 00:29:33,420
You'll see a darker wine color to the pork and more marbling,

438
00:29:33,421 --> 00:29:35,420
and marbling is intramuscular fat.

439
00:29:35,421 --> 00:29:37,420
That's the obvious difference.

440
00:29:37,421 --> 00:29:42,420
And when you taste it, there's a lot of conclusive data

441
00:29:42,421 --> 00:29:45,420
that shows the difference in taste for that pork.

442
00:29:46,420 --> 00:29:49,420
Becker's latest old-world experiment is a bit nutty,

443
00:29:49,421 --> 00:29:54,420
specifically acorns for the famous Italian ham prosciutto.

444
00:29:54,421 --> 00:29:57,420
The tradition of feeding acorns to pigs

445
00:29:57,421 --> 00:30:00,420
goes back through medieval times,

446
00:30:00,421 --> 00:30:04,420
and we have recipes as far back as Caesar campaigning in Gaul

447
00:30:04,421 --> 00:30:07,420
and his troops carrying prosciutto

448
00:30:07,421 --> 00:30:09,420
that's been finished with acorns.

449
00:30:09,421 --> 00:30:11,420
The rear legs of these acorn-noshing pigs

450
00:30:11,421 --> 00:30:14,420
will soon be transformed into upscale prosciutto ham,

451
00:30:14,421 --> 00:30:18,420
downstate at La Quertia in Norwalk, Iowa.

452
00:30:18,421 --> 00:30:25,583
Iowa. La Quertia is one of a handful of artisan American prosciutto makers.

453
00:30:25,584 --> 00:30:26,920
The first step

454
00:30:26,921 --> 00:30:29,800
in curing it is to remove moisture.

455
00:30:29,801 --> 00:30:36,460
We apply salt externally, we don't tumble it, we don't inject it, we don't cook it,

456
00:30:36,461 --> 00:30:39,526
we don't spice it, we don't ferment it.

457
00:30:39,527 --> 00:30:43,300
We just put the salt on and then we use movement

458
00:30:43,301 --> 00:30:49,240
through temperatures to allow the salt to penetrate and the flavor to develop in the ham.

459
00:30:49,640 --> 00:30:52,540
We use the refrigeration to take the moisture out of the meat.

460
00:30:53,580 --> 00:30:58,100
We move it from a very rapid drying situation to a much slower drying situation.

461
00:30:59,380 --> 00:31:04,300
In the centuries prior to refrigeration, prosciutto makers used the changing seasons

462
00:31:04,301 --> 00:31:06,440
to preserve and then dry cure the ham.

463
00:31:07,740 --> 00:31:10,120
By slaughtering and salting in the frigid winter

464
00:31:10,121 --> 00:31:13,140
and then drying and aging through the spring and summer.

465
00:31:13,300 --> 00:31:19,920
When the salt is applied, the cold inhibits bacterial activity, giving time for the salt

466
00:31:19,921 --> 00:31:23,700
to fully penetrate and push out moisture.

467
00:31:23,701 --> 00:31:28,920
The salt protects the meat from bacteria as it slowly dries, shrinking up to two-thirds

468
00:31:28,921 --> 00:31:32,900
of its original size.

469
00:31:32,901 --> 00:31:39,920
After approximately 10 months of aging, the hams are trimmed, packaged, and shipped.

470
00:31:39,921 --> 00:31:42,460
While the price is as high as $30 a pound,

471
00:31:42,461 --> 00:31:46,620
it's a gourmet delight for discerning ham lovers in the know.

472
00:31:46,621 --> 00:31:51,720
I always like to say the British mariners ate salt pork,

473
00:31:51,721 --> 00:31:54,580
and the Roman legionnaires ate prosciutto.

474
00:31:54,581 --> 00:31:57,320
You know, who do you want to be?

475
00:31:57,321 --> 00:32:00,960
With little thought, we take this versatile animal's life

476
00:32:00,961 --> 00:32:04,260
to concoct countless mouth-watering edibles.

477
00:32:04,261 --> 00:32:06,920
But one day, when you least expect it,

478
00:32:06,921 --> 00:32:12,520
The pig may just save your life.

479
00:32:12,521 --> 00:32:16,200
In 1995, one of the worst hog waste disasters occurred

480
00:32:16,201 --> 00:32:20,480
in North Carolina when a lagoon dike holding 25 million gallons

481
00:32:20,481 --> 00:32:23,980
of liquid pig waste collapsed and found its way

482
00:32:23,981 --> 00:32:25,980
into the new river.

483
00:32:25,981 --> 00:32:29,000
This event greatly influenced ongoing environmental reform

484
00:32:29,001 --> 00:32:31,060
in the pork industry.

485
00:32:31,061 --> 00:32:33,780
The pig will return on Modern Marvels.

486
00:32:33,781 --> 00:32:42,780
Every day, millions of people worldwide count on the pig as an important source of protein.

487
00:32:42,781 --> 00:32:48,780
But along with feeding us, he's saving our lives in ways few could ever have imagined.

488
00:32:48,781 --> 00:32:51,780
We're short of human organ donors.

489
00:32:51,781 --> 00:32:55,780
But today, science holds great hope for xenotransplantation,

490
00:32:55,781 --> 00:33:01,780
or the process of taking an organ from an animal and transplanting it into a human.

491
00:33:01,781 --> 00:33:07,540
Presently, science is counting on the pig, an animal whose organs and circulatory system

492
00:33:07,541 --> 00:33:10,020
are strikingly similar to a human's.

493
00:33:10,021 --> 00:33:15,880
The advantage of pigs is that their physiology is actually pretty close to humans, surprisingly.

494
00:33:15,881 --> 00:33:21,340
For example, insulin, pig insulin was used before human insulin was available for diabetics,

495
00:33:21,341 --> 00:33:26,020
and it differs by just a single amino acid from the human insulin.

496
00:33:26,021 --> 00:33:31,420
While medical science still has a long way to go before doctors can routinely transplant

497
00:33:31,421 --> 00:33:33,006
pig organs into humans.

498
00:33:33,007 --> 00:33:37,420
It's much closer to developing a life-saving use for certain pig

499
00:33:37,421 --> 00:33:38,121
cells.

500
00:33:38,122 --> 00:33:45,220
Approximately one million people with type 1 diabetes lack pancreatic islet cells

501
00:33:45,221 --> 00:33:48,512
that the body needs to metabolize sugar.

502
00:33:48,513 --> 00:33:53,120
Without these cells, long-term complications like kidney

503
00:33:53,121 --> 00:33:55,547
disease and blindness can occur.

504
00:33:55,548 --> 00:34:00,400
At Chimerics in Blair, Nebraska, the race is on to perfect a way

505
00:34:00,401 --> 00:34:03,120
to transfer these pancreatic cells from pig to human

506
00:34:03,121 --> 00:34:05,960
and forever end insulin dependence.

507
00:34:06,860 --> 00:34:11,460
This is a pancreas from a pig that we removed a couple hours ago.

508
00:34:12,420 --> 00:34:17,780
And what we plan to do today is to isolate the pancreatic islet cell.

509
00:34:18,000 --> 00:34:21,620
And this is what we hope to eventually transplant into patients with diabetes

510
00:34:21,621 --> 00:34:25,500
to allow them to metabolize their glucose better.

511
00:34:25,501 --> 00:34:34,720
The challenge is to perfect a method to transplant the pig's islet cells so that a human's white blood cells won't destroy the foreign pig cells.

512
00:34:36,380 --> 00:34:40,600
Scientists cut the pancreas into fine pieces and isolate the islets.

513
00:34:42,180 --> 00:34:44,740
Later, they'll be cryopreserved until needed.

514
00:34:46,040 --> 00:34:51,520
When clinical testing is completed, and if a procedure is proven safe and effective for humans,

515
00:34:52,340 --> 00:34:55,340
type 1 diabetics may have a cure within the next decade.

516
00:34:55,500 --> 00:35:03,720
While diabetics play the waiting game, some 80,000 Americans already know the pig can mend

517
00:35:03,721 --> 00:35:04,757
a broken heart.

518
00:35:04,758 --> 00:35:10,220
Sterilized and molded porcine heart valves are frequent replacements for faulty

519
00:35:10,221 --> 00:35:11,081
human valves.

520
00:35:11,082 --> 00:35:16,580
The human aortic heart valve opens and closes about 70 times per minute in an adult

521
00:35:16,581 --> 00:35:21,340
male, regulating blood flow as it is pumped from the heart to the rest of the body.

522
00:35:21,341 --> 00:35:29,540
Surgeons implant a porcine valve if the human valve contains a defect or simply wears out with

523
00:35:29,541 --> 00:35:34,320
age. The pig's valve can last more than 25 years.

524
00:35:34,321 --> 00:35:39,100
One reason why more than 500,000 people worldwide

525
00:35:39,101 --> 00:35:40,801
have porcine valves.

526
00:35:40,802 --> 00:35:46,840
Compared to other animals, the pig's aortic valve is amazingly durable.

527
00:35:46,841 --> 00:35:53,100
The same goes for his cast iron intestine, a fact that is turning millions of people

528
00:35:53,101 --> 00:35:55,740
with severe injuries into big fans of the pig.

529
00:35:57,260 --> 00:35:59,560
In this case, it's kind of a pig patch kit.

530
00:36:02,980 --> 00:36:08,840
Cook Biotech in West Lafayette, Indiana, manufactures a product called Surgisys Biodesign,

531
00:36:09,500 --> 00:36:15,260
taken directly from a pig's small intestine submucosa, a thin layer of strong connective

532
00:36:15,261 --> 00:36:15,961
tissue.

533
00:36:16,840 --> 00:36:23,200
Once sterilized and processed, it's placed directly onto human tissue, where it can help

534
00:36:23,201 --> 00:36:29,720
heal dozens of ailments, from severe wounds and burns to hernias, and tissue repairs in

535
00:36:29,721 --> 00:36:31,200
formerly conjoined twins.

536
00:36:31,201 --> 00:36:37,640
For a long time, surgeons have been looking for ways to repair the body's soft tissue.

537
00:36:37,641 --> 00:36:43,160
And in many cases, they've put metals and they've put plastics and even more exotic

538
00:36:43,161 --> 00:36:45,760
composite and polymer materials.

539
00:36:45,761 --> 00:36:50,300
What they really wanted to do was put a biologic material in

540
00:36:50,301 --> 00:36:53,400
that would actually respond to the body and over time

541
00:36:53,401 --> 00:36:56,700
allow the body to heal itself.

542
00:36:56,701 --> 00:36:59,680
The biodesign acts as a three-dimensional matrix,

543
00:36:59,681 --> 00:37:03,920
or a scaffold, for the human tissue to remodel itself.

544
00:37:03,921 --> 00:37:07,380
When implanted, the human tissues deliver cells and nutrients,

545
00:37:07,381 --> 00:37:11,860
encouraging capillaries to grow and rebuild the damaged site.

546
00:37:11,861 --> 00:37:15,020
Then after it has been used, it actually goes away,

547
00:37:15,021 --> 00:37:20,680
which is nice, and now you leave behind a nice, strong repair that's truly human tissue.

548
00:37:21,860 --> 00:37:28,300
Inside the Cook Biotech labs, technicians cut and fashion the biodesign material in many shapes and sizes,

549
00:37:28,980 --> 00:37:31,660
depending on the geometry and purpose of the patient's need.

550
00:37:34,880 --> 00:37:38,300
Cook also inspects the material to catch imperfections.

551
00:37:39,060 --> 00:37:42,780
Before it's approved for use, technicians run a battery of tests,

552
00:37:42,781 --> 00:37:46,440
including one for durability that repeatedly stretches the material,

553
00:37:47,540 --> 00:37:50,320
simulating the repetitive physical forces in the human body.

554
00:37:53,260 --> 00:37:55,340
When all quality checks are completed,

555
00:37:56,080 --> 00:37:59,260
technicians heat seal the biodesign material in sterile packages

556
00:37:59,261 --> 00:38:01,560
and distribute it throughout the world.

557
00:38:02,220 --> 00:38:05,660
A pig patch kit with the power to jumpstart healing

558
00:38:05,661 --> 00:38:07,760
in virtually all regions of the human body.

559
00:38:08,480 --> 00:38:11,300
I think in the future, the pig will be a source

560
00:38:11,301 --> 00:38:13,880
for a lot of different matrix materials.

561
00:38:14,100 --> 00:38:16,660
It also could lead to the delivery of drugs,

562
00:38:16,820 --> 00:38:19,940
the ability to repair tissues all over the body,

563
00:38:20,020 --> 00:38:22,380
even tissues that we haven't even thought of repairing now.

564
00:38:24,120 --> 00:38:27,300
Although the pig's future in medicine offers great promise,

565
00:38:28,280 --> 00:38:30,760
some aren't as interested in transplanting its organs

566
00:38:30,761 --> 00:38:33,100
as much as they are in sautéing them

567
00:38:33,101 --> 00:38:35,560
with mushrooms, capers, and lemon.

568
00:38:37,860 --> 00:38:40,800
In 1992, doctors at Duke University

569
00:38:40,801 --> 00:38:43,060
were the first to successfully attach a pig liver

570
00:38:43,061 --> 00:38:45,740
to a dying patient to buy time

571
00:38:45,741 --> 00:38:47,120
until a human liver was available.

572
00:38:47,900 --> 00:38:50,960
A series of five pig livers lasted three days

573
00:38:50,961 --> 00:38:54,300
until a human liver transplant saved the patient's life.

574
00:38:54,880 --> 00:38:57,560
The pig will return on Modern Marvels.

575
00:39:02,180 --> 00:39:03,560
When we look at the pig,

576
00:39:04,080 --> 00:39:05,880
most of us see bacon for breakfast

577
00:39:05,881 --> 00:39:07,060
and pork chops for dinner.

578
00:39:08,260 --> 00:39:10,740
It may be hard to also visualize tires

579
00:39:10,741 --> 00:39:16,180
cosmetics, glue, shoes, and hundreds of other products.

580
00:39:17,260 --> 00:39:21,520
Yet in each, nearly one-third of our porcine pal is a basic ingredient.

581
00:39:23,040 --> 00:39:27,760
Fat becomes lard and may find its way into lipstick, paint, or crayons.

582
00:39:29,160 --> 00:39:33,500
Scrap tissue is reduced to protein meal and may become pet food or chicken feed.

583
00:39:34,540 --> 00:39:39,840
Bone, when cooked down, excretes gelatin that helps produce x-rays and film.

584
00:39:40,740 --> 00:39:43,880
Even the organs and tidbits, like the tongue and the feet,

585
00:39:43,881 --> 00:39:45,780
collectively known as offal,

586
00:39:45,781 --> 00:39:49,480
are a prized food source in Asia, Europe, and other continents.

587
00:39:49,481 --> 00:39:51,780
We don't use a lot of the offal products,

588
00:39:51,781 --> 00:39:54,880
and we are able to ship them to countries that do utilize them

589
00:39:54,881 --> 00:39:56,580
and are willing to pay for them,

590
00:39:56,581 --> 00:39:59,300
and it makes up a fairly substantial percentage

591
00:39:59,301 --> 00:40:02,500
of the actual profit for the animals that are being shipped.

592
00:40:02,501 --> 00:40:06,100
A handful of trailblazing chefs in America

593
00:40:06,101 --> 00:40:09,200
are trying to serve up odd pig parts to the rest of us.

594
00:40:10,740 --> 00:40:13,440
At Encanto Restaurant in San Francisco,

595
00:40:14,220 --> 00:40:15,440
the house specialty is awful,

596
00:40:16,240 --> 00:40:22,480
like tongue, brain, heart, liver, kidney, feet, and intestine,

597
00:40:23,180 --> 00:40:25,600
or chitlins, call it Back to the Future.

598
00:40:26,340 --> 00:40:29,640
My grandparents' generation was a firm believer in using it

599
00:40:29,641 --> 00:40:31,820
because they were of the World War II generation.

600
00:40:32,400 --> 00:40:33,820
You know, they had the rationing stamps,

601
00:40:33,821 --> 00:40:36,960
and they could go further with buying a tongue

602
00:40:36,961 --> 00:40:39,980
than they could with a pork chop or a pork loin.

603
00:40:40,740 --> 00:40:43,820
In the Encanto kitchen, it all starts from the top.

604
00:40:44,320 --> 00:40:46,740
What we're going to do is debone this pig's head

605
00:40:46,741 --> 00:40:48,320
and set it up for porchetta detesta,

606
00:40:48,640 --> 00:40:51,540
and then I will remove the brain from the skull.

607
00:40:52,280 --> 00:40:54,720
It takes just a little patience to debone a pig's head.

608
00:40:54,800 --> 00:41:00,480
It's not that labor-intensive if you're accustomed to it.

609
00:41:00,580 --> 00:41:03,740
I got a good crack in there now, so she's going to...

610
00:41:03,741 --> 00:41:05,120
There she goes.

611
00:41:06,720 --> 00:41:08,120
What did you do yesterday, honey?

612
00:41:08,121 --> 00:41:09,560
Oh, no, nothing big.

613
00:41:09,561 --> 00:41:11,260
I just split a pizza in half.

614
00:41:11,261 --> 00:41:14,560
The brain is too soft and tender to actually sear.

615
00:41:14,561 --> 00:41:15,760
So we're gonna poach it.

616
00:41:15,761 --> 00:41:17,260
You want a nice gentle poach.

617
00:41:17,261 --> 00:41:18,760
Okay?

618
00:41:18,761 --> 00:41:20,600
So I'm just gonna take that brain, I'm not seasoning it,

619
00:41:20,601 --> 00:41:22,100
I'm just gonna drop it right in.

620
00:41:22,101 --> 00:41:23,100
Ooh.

621
00:41:23,101 --> 00:41:24,100
Ever so gently.

622
00:41:24,101 --> 00:41:26,720
So, brain's been poached ever so delicately.

623
00:41:26,721 --> 00:41:29,720
I'm gonna let that sit there and cool.

624
00:41:29,721 --> 00:41:31,620
So now it's nice and cool, okay?

625
00:41:31,621 --> 00:41:34,220
So I'm gonna season that a little salt.

626
00:41:34,221 --> 00:41:35,360
Fresh ground pepper.

627
00:41:35,361 --> 00:41:37,560
The nuttiness and earthiness of the mushrooms

628
00:41:37,561 --> 00:41:40,600
will really contrast very well with the brain.

629
00:41:40,601 --> 00:41:44,400
So I'm gonna add a piece of butter,

630
00:41:44,401 --> 00:41:45,860
and now I'm gonna add the brain.

631
00:41:45,861 --> 00:41:48,900
Right where that butter is, it's gonna help caramelize.

632
00:41:48,901 --> 00:41:51,080
Give a nice nutty flavor to it, okay?

633
00:41:51,081 --> 00:41:53,000
Eating brains will not make you smarter.

634
00:41:53,001 --> 00:41:55,540
I've actually been asked that a couple times.

635
00:41:55,541 --> 00:41:57,580
Are we gonna be smarter today? No, not really.

636
00:41:57,581 --> 00:41:59,240
We're gonna come right over here.

637
00:41:59,241 --> 00:42:00,920
See that brain right there?

638
00:42:00,921 --> 00:42:03,560
So we have these beautiful chanterelle mushrooms

639
00:42:03,561 --> 00:42:07,320
with capers and lemon brown butter.

640
00:42:07,321 --> 00:42:13,320
And the piste resistance is your fresh pork brain right there.

641
00:42:13,321 --> 00:42:17,320
We'll finish that off with a little bit of its sauce.

642
00:42:17,321 --> 00:42:22,320
It may not be brains like your grandfather do them,

643
00:42:22,321 --> 00:42:24,320
but over the last two centuries,

644
00:42:24,321 --> 00:42:28,320
many Americans were accustomed to pitching in on slaughtering day.

645
00:42:28,321 --> 00:42:34,320
Then you would have all the off-fall cuts would be turned into something like a pâtés or terrines.

646
00:42:34,321 --> 00:42:37,220
Sausages would be made, legs would be turned into hams,

647
00:42:37,221 --> 00:42:39,220
city hams and salt-cured hams,

648
00:42:39,221 --> 00:42:41,020
and the head would be made into head cheese.

649
00:42:41,021 --> 00:42:43,720
This was a normal way of life in this country

650
00:42:43,721 --> 00:42:46,360
just as much as it was all throughout Europe.

651
00:42:46,361 --> 00:42:49,960
Encanto has evolved the family farm ritual,

652
00:42:49,961 --> 00:42:52,080
into oat cuisine.

653
00:42:52,081 --> 00:42:54,080
How about some kidney with lentils?

654
00:42:54,081 --> 00:42:57,180
Or trotter cakes, a new spin on pigs' feet?

655
00:42:57,181 --> 00:42:58,880
Save room for dessert.

656
00:42:58,881 --> 00:43:03,720
So what we've done is we've taken this great pork blood here

657
00:43:03,721 --> 00:43:06,060
and we've made a final dessert.

658
00:43:06,520 --> 00:43:09,560
And it's a chocolate blood panna cotta with ancient spice,

659
00:43:09,680 --> 00:43:11,900
which is poivlon and cinnamon and cloves,

660
00:43:12,040 --> 00:43:13,560
so almost like your Christmassy spices.

661
00:43:14,320 --> 00:43:16,140
Blood was a very traditional thickener

662
00:43:16,141 --> 00:43:18,360
for sauces and desserts back in the day,

663
00:43:18,480 --> 00:43:20,260
and this one is always a big surprise.

664
00:43:20,640 --> 00:43:22,380
You would never know there's blood in there unless I told you.

665
00:43:24,040 --> 00:43:26,540
Encanto serves a monthly head-to-tail dinner

666
00:43:26,541 --> 00:43:27,740
that's a regular sellout.

667
00:43:28,180 --> 00:43:30,480
There's the direct correlation to oneself where they say,

668
00:43:30,481 --> 00:43:31,720
oh, I can't eat tongue, I have one,

669
00:43:31,760 --> 00:43:32,880
or I can't eat heart, I have one.

670
00:43:32,881 --> 00:43:36,720
So it's getting people to just be willing to try it for the first time,

671
00:43:36,721 --> 00:43:38,720
and I think that's when you change their opinion.

672
00:43:38,721 --> 00:43:44,480
Most of us may not be serving up that blood and chocolate panna cotta anytime soon,

673
00:43:44,481 --> 00:43:49,040
but there's little doubt that the pig will be on our dinner tables for years to come.

674
00:43:49,041 --> 00:43:54,240
Though technology has transformed him into a walking meat factory,

675
00:43:54,241 --> 00:43:58,000
and science has enlisted him to heal our ailments,

676
00:43:58,001 --> 00:44:01,040
the pig will always help us.

